course: dictionary
lesson: 4112
title: Glad: happy, willing and thankful
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Glad means happy and pleased about something. «Glad to meet you» is a polite, friendly thing to say when you first meet someone.
explain: Glad is usually used after words like be and feel: «I'd be glad to help» and «I'm glad you came». You would not usually put it straight before a noun, so you say a happy dog, not a glad dog.
explain: Glad can also mean thankful that something happened. «Toad is always glad to see you and always sorry when you go» shows that glad is a warm, friendly feeling. The words around glad tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Glad means happy and pleased about something.
fact: Glad can mean very willing to do something: «I'd be glad to help».
fact: Glad can mean thankful that something happened: «I'm glad you're safe».
fact: «Gladly» is the adverb. You do something gladly when you do it in a happy, willing way.
fact: In Chinese, «gāoxìng» means glad or pleased, and it is a little more grown-up.
exercise: «Glad to meet you» is a polite thing to say when you first meet someone. What does glad mean here? | Angry and cross | *Happy and pleased | Tired and sleepy | Lost and alone
exercise: «I'd be glad to help.» What does glad show? | Angry | Frightened | Bored | *Very willing to do something
exercise: Which word is the adverb of glad, meaning in a happy, willing way? | gladness | *gladly | gladder
test: «I'm glad you came.» In this sentence, glad means what? | Bored | *Pleased that something happened | Hungry | Sorry
test: Which sentence uses glad in the right place, after a word like be or feel? | Glad boy smiled. | *The boy was glad to win. | The glad boy smiled.
exam: Toad says, «I am always glad to see you.» Why does the writer use glad? | *It shows warm, friendly happiness | It shows Toad is lost | It shows Toad is cross | It shows Toad is hungry
